{
  "id": "uniqueness-constraints-for-experiment-metrics-and-results",
  "title": "Uniqueness constraints for experiment metrics and results",
  "kind": "invariant",
  "answers": [
    "What must be unique for experiment metrics?",
    "What is the ExperimentMetricResult key?",
    "Why does timeseries sync offset by 1 second?"
  ],
  "body": "- metric.uuid unique across primary, secondary and saved-metric uuids per experiment: `validate_no_duplicate_metric_uuids`; `_assign_uuids_to_metrics` seeds `seen` from `_collect_saved_metric_uuids`, regenerates collisions and fixes ordering via `_remap_ordering`. Clones: `_regenerate_all_metric_uuids`.\n- `ExperimentMetricResult` unique_together (experiment, metric_uuid, query_to) in `models/experiment.py`; fingerprint NOT in key, so writers use `update_or_create` with fingerprint in defaults (different fp overwrites in place): `temporal/recalculation_logic.py`, `timeseries_sync.py:_copy_points`.\n- `_copy_points` writes at newest_point + `_WINDOW_OFFSET` (1s); writing at the point's own query_to would rewrite the timeseries row's fingerprint.\n- Reading a run: match recalc fingerprint AND query_to == recalc.query_to (`recalculation.py:get_run_results`), since one config accumulates a row per query_to.\n- One active timeseries recalc per (experiment, fingerprint) (`unique_active_recalculation_per_experiment_metric`); one active metrics recalc per experiment (`unique_active_metrics_recalculation_per_experiment`).",
  "applies": "Backend experiments product.",
